<!-- EDUCATION -->

<section class="pathway-section" id="education">

<div class="pathway-header">

<div class="pathway-number">01</div>

<div>

<h2>
Traditional Education Intelligence
</h2>

<p>
Do not think of education as simply paying tuition,
attending classes, and receiving a degree.
Institutions contain multiple financial, academic,
professional, and informational systems.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="lesson">

<div class="lesson-label">
Overlooked Strategy
</div>

<h3>
The institution itself is a resource network.
</h3>

<p>
Students often search only for scholarships.
But universities can also contain departmental awards,
research opportunities, faculty relationships, tutoring,
career centers, alumni networks, student employment,
competitions, professional organizations, and specialized programs.
</p>

</div>


<div class="resource-cards">

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🏛 Department-Level Money</h4>
<p>
Look beyond university-wide scholarships.
Departments may offer awards, travel funding,
research support, conference funding, and
discipline-specific opportunities.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🔬 Faculty Research</h4>
<p>
Professors sometimes need research assistants,
data collection, literature reviews, technical assistance,
or project support.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🎤 Academic Competitions</h4>
<p>
Case competitions, writing contests, innovation challenges,
hackathons, and research showcases can create evidence
and connections.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🤝 Alumni Intelligence</h4>
<p>
Alumni can provide information about industries,
hiring patterns, internships, companies, and skills.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🧭 Transfer Agreements</h4>
<p>
Community colleges and universities may have structured
transfer pathways that reduce cost or simplify progression.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>📚 Academic Support</h4>
<p>
Tutoring, writing centers, libraries, career advising,
and workshops can improve performance without private services.
</p>
</div>

</div>


<br>


<div class="two-column">

<div class="resource-panel">

<h3>
Questions Most Applicants Never Ask
</h3>

<ul>

<li>Which departments have their own funding?</li>

<li>What research projects need assistants?</li>

<li>Which courses have employer connections?</li>

<li>What competitions create public evidence?</li>

<li>Which alumni work in my target industry?</li>

<li>What campus jobs build transferable skills?</li>

<li>Are there accelerated or transfer pathways?</li>

<li>What professional associations does the school fund?</li>

</ul>

</div>


<div class="resource-panel">

<h3>
Institutional Leverage Map
</h3>

<ul>

<li>Financial aid office → funding intelligence</li>

<li>Academic department → specialized opportunities</li>

<li>Career center → employer intelligence</li>

<li>Faculty → research and mentorship</li>

<li>Alumni office → relationship access</li>

<li>Library → information infrastructure</li>

<li>Student organizations → leadership evidence</li>

<li>Career fairs → market feedback</li>

</ul>

</div>

</div>

</section>


<!-- SELF EDUCATION -->

<section class="pathway-section" id="selfeducation">

<div class="pathway-header">

<div class="pathway-number">02</div>

<div>

<h2>
Self-Education Intelligence
</h2>

<p>
The problem with self-education is rarely access to information.
The problem is converting information into credible evidence.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="lesson">

<div class="lesson-label">
Higher-Level Lesson
</div>

<h3>
Learning has economic value when it changes what you can demonstrate.
</h3>

<p>
Completing a course proves that you completed a course.
A functioning project demonstrates application.
A case study demonstrates reasoning.
A measurable result demonstrates impact.
</p>

<br>

<p>
The strongest self-education system moves through:
<strong>
consumption → application → evidence → visibility.
</strong>
</p>

</div>


<div class="resource-cards">

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🧪 Build Before You Feel Ready</h4>
<p>
Create small projects while learning.
Waiting until the end of a course delays evidence production.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>📁 Create a Proof Portfolio</h4>
<p>
Keep screenshots, explanations, before-and-after results,
decisions made, and lessons learned.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>📊 Measure Your Projects</h4>
<p>
Track time saved, errors reduced, conversions improved,
processes simplified, or users helped.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🧠 Reconstruct Real Problems</h4>
<p>
Take public business problems and create your own analysis,
dashboard, website, workflow, or solution.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🔎 Reverse Engineer Job Descriptions</h4>
<p>
Extract recurring skills from job postings and build projects
specifically designed to demonstrate those skills.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🌐 Publish Learning Artifacts</h4>
<p>
Tutorials, case studies, GitHub projects, reports,
dashboards, and demonstrations make invisible learning visible.
</p>
</div>

</div>


<br>


